return array (
  'content' => '<p>Custom architectural metalwork — PVD partitions, entrance doors, railings, Corten screens and furniture — requires clearer specifications than catalogue joinery. Indian architects who document finish samples, fixing assumptions and coordination milestones reduce site conflict and change orders. This guide outlines what to put on drawings and when to involve the fabricator.</p><h2>Early engagement vs late procurement</h2><p>Involve metal fabricator during schematic design when floor plates, double-height volumes and entrance axes are set. Late addition of full-height PVD partitions often conflicts with already cast slab edges or MEP drops.</p><p>Vyomika Atelier collaborates with architects from our Delhi studio on pan-India projects via the <a href="/professionals">professionals page</a>.</p><h2>Drawing set essentials</h2><p>Plan dimensions to finished surfaces, elevation heights, opening widths, swing directions, glass type notes, PVD finish sample reference, fixing method (floor/ceiling/wall), and coordination with electrical for door interfaces.</p><p>Reference studio pages for scope language: <a href="/studio/pvd-partitions">PVD partitions</a>, <a href="/studio/main-entrance-pvd-doors">entrance doors</a>, <a href="/railings">railings</a>, <a href="/corten-steel">Corten</a>.</p><h2>Finish schedules</h2><p>One line per visible metal element: location, substrate, PVD colour name from approved sample, surface prep (hairline/mirror), orientation of brush direction on adjacent panels.</p><p>Cross-link hardware on <a href="/shop/door-handles">door handles</a> with same finish code.</p><h2>Structural and MEP coordination</h2><p>Confirm slab penetration policy with structural engineer. Ceiling-mounted partition tracks need clear services path in BOQ stage.</p><p>Floor drain locations near entrance frames affect threshold detail — note on door schedule.</p><h2>Performance language without overclaiming</h2><p>State required glass safety type, guard height intent and exposure class (interior/exterior) without inventing test certificates. Refer compliance to project consultant of record.</p><h2>Tender and substitution</h2><p>Define approved fabricator or performance spec to prevent low bids that omit PVD batch matching or correct glass thickness. Substitution clause should require sample approval.</p><p>Process overview: <a href="/blog/drawing-to-installation-custom-metal-fabrication-process">drawing to installation</a>.</p><h2>BOQ and scope boundaries</h2><p>Separate supply-only metalwork from install-inclusive packages in tender BOQ. Ambiguous scope causes low bids that omit batch-matched PVD or correct glass thickness.</p><p>List exclusions clearly: core drilling, scaffolding, temporary protection, stone patching after fixings — or include with allowance.</p><h2>Interface details with other trades</h2><p>Show how partition tracks meet ceiling gypsum, stone floor and curtain pockets. MEP routes above full-height glass need resolution before ceiling close-in.</p><p>Entrance door thresholds interface with floor drains and external paving — note on door schedule with section detail.</p><h2>Performance language without overclaiming</h2><p>State glass safety type, guard height intent and exposure class without inventing test certificates. Compliance remains with project consultant of record.</p><p>Refer statutory compliance to the project consultant of record — do not cite regional regulations unless project-specific documentation exists.</p><h2>Review milestones</h2><p>Schematic: system type and finish direction. DD: dimensions and fixing approach. CD: shop drawing approval and sample sign-off before fabrication release.</p><p>International enquiries are reviewed project by project — export scope confirmed separately from domestic supply.</p><h2>Schedule structure on drawing sets</h2><p>Create a metalwork schedule listing item reference, location, substrate, finish sample code, glass type, fixing method and responsible contractor. Cross-reference partition types, door numbers and railing zones so tenderers cannot omit scope slices.</p><h2>Substitution and mock-up clauses</h2><p>Require approval before substituting glass thickness, PVD batch or fixing hardware. Mock-up panels for high-visibility lobbies reduce dispute at handover — one full-size sample beats A4 finish chips alone.</p><h3>Defects and acceptable variation</h3><p>Natural materials and directional hairline finishes show acceptable variation within a batch. Document what constitutes reject-level mismatch versus normal manufacturing tolerance so snagging does not stall on subjective preferences.</p><h2>Coordination meetings worth holding</h2><p>Hold a short metalwork coordination session after ceiling and MEP routes are frozen but before gypsum close-in. Review partition head details, door threshold interfaces and Corten runoff paths with landscape if applicable.</p><h2>Information fabricators need in the first enquiry</h2><p>Early packages should include PDF plans, elevation photos, intended finish direction, approximate opening sizes and project city. This allows feasibility feedback before detailed documentation is complete — saving redraw cycles when a partition type cannot fit existing structure.</p><p>For process timing after specification is frozen, see <a href="/blog/drawing-to-installation-custom-metal-fabrication-process">drawing to installation workflow</a>. International enquiries are reviewed project by project.</p><h2>Contractor briefing before site start</h2><p>Issue approved shop drawings to fixing contractors with explicit notes on substrate preparation, anchor types and sequence relative to ceiling and floor finishes. A short pre-install briefing prevents improvised fixings that void intended load paths on glass partitions and door frames.</p><p>Include fabricator contact for technical queries rather than routing all questions through the architect during busy site phases.</p><h2>Reviewing contractor submittals</h2><p>When contractors submit alternative metal suppliers, compare finish sample batches and glass make-up against the approved specification — not only price. Substitution without sample review is the most common source of colour drift at handover on partition packages.</p><h2>Handover documentation for facilities teams</h2><p>Include cleaning guidance, finish batch codes and fabricator contact in O&M folders — facilities teams replace handles and patch partitions years after architect handover. Clear records reduce mismatched repairs on large residential and hospitality projects.</p><p>Request fabricator method statements for unusual fixings — cantilevered glass partitions and heavy entrance leaves may need details beyond generic partition notes on GA drawings.</p><h2>Tender clarification meetings</h2><p>Short clarification meetings with shortlisted fabricators reduce ambiguous scope before award — especially for glass thickness, PVD batch matching and Corten runoff detailing. Document answers as addenda to the specification rather than relying on verbal assurances alone.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Architects specify custom metalwork successfully with early fabricator input, finish schedules tied to physical samples, and drawings that show fixing and glass — not just elevation aesthetics. Treat metal as primary architecture, not a shop drawing afterthought.</p><p>Start at <a href="/professionals">professionals</a>.</p><p>See process in <a href="/blog/drawing-to-installation-custom-metal-fabrication-process">fabrication process guide</a>.</p><p>Browse <a href="/projects">projects</a> for specification references.</p><p>For a project-specific quotation, share floor plans or photos through our <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. Architects and interior designers may also begin from the <a href="/professionals">professionals collaboration page</a>.</p>',
